WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.635
Jeff Geerling now

00:00:01.447 --> 00:00:02.437
serves time to

00:00:02.249 --> 00:00:03.062
the world

00:00:03.582 --> 00:00:04.796
Jeff Geerling has

00:00:04.608 --> 00:00:05.563
joined the global

00:00:05.374 --> 00:00:06.541
NTP Pool with

00:00:06.353 --> 00:00:07.473
a public Stratum

00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:08.310
2 time server

00:00:08.511 --> 00:00:09.230
fed by his

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:10.126
own Stratum 1

00:00:09.938 --> 00:00:11.011
Raspberry Pi in

00:00:10.823 --> 00:00:11.648
the studio

00:00:12.166 --> 00:00:13.156
That Pi uses

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.052
a TimeHAT and

00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:15.279
a timing-grade u-blox

00:00:15.091 --> 00:00:16.517
GNSS module with

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:17.496
an outdoor antenna

00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:18.533
while a separate

00:00:18.345 --> 00:00:19.571
public server keeps

00:00:19.383 --> 00:00:20.361
his home address

00:00:20.173 --> 00:00:20.904
private

00:00:21.422 --> 00:00:22.330
His advice

00:00:22.436 --> 00:00:23.061
you don't need

00:00:22.872 --> 00:00:24.252
GNSS to contribute

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:24.995
but you do

00:00:24.806 --> 00:00:26.197
need long-term uptime

00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:27.388
good time and

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.297
a stable IP

00:00:28.415 --> 00:00:29.617
because traffic can

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:30.608
linger for years

00:00:31.134 --> 00:00:32.148
Full story on

00:00:31.959 --> 00:00:32.855
the dashboard
